<<<
+ not for-. On the other hand the noun for(e)bears, & foregoing & foregone in the foregoing list, a foregone conclusion, contain the ordinary fore,
===
+ not for-. On the other hand the noun *for(e)bears*, & *foregoing* & *foregone* in *the foregoing list*, *a foregone conclusion*, contain the ordinary *fore*,
>>>

<<<
& should be spelt with the e, & pronounced for- or for-, not fer-. Foreclose & forfeit contain another prefix
===
& should be spelt with the *e*, & pronounced fo͞r- or for-, not *fer-*. *Foreclose* & *forfeit* contain another prefix
>>>

<<<
again (L foris outside), though foreclose has had its spelling affected by natural confusion with English fore.
===
again (L *foris* outside), though *foreclose* has had its spelling affected by natural confusion with English *fore*.
>>>

<<<
All the words, whether established or made for the occasion, compounded with fore, as forebode, forewarn, foreman, fore-ordained, are spelt with the e & should have the
===
All the words, whether established or made for the occasion, compounded with *fore*, as *forebode, forewarn, foreman, fore-ordained*, are spelt with the *e* & should have the
>>>

<<<
for or for sound distinct.
===
fo͞r or for sound distinct.
>>>

<<<
forgather. 1. For the spelling see FOR-, FORE-. 2. The word is a Scotticism not quite at home in English, as appears in Many interesting people he met at Lady St Helier’s, where Mr Chamberlain,
===
**forgather.** 1. For the spelling see ^^FOR-^^, ^^FORE-^^. 2. The word is a Scotticism not quite at home in English, as appears in *Many interesting people he met at Lady St Helier's, where Mr Chamberlain,
>>>

<<<
among others, foregathered. It may perhaps be said that whenever the verb is not, as it usually is, in the plural, it is followed by with.
===
among others, foregathered.* It may perhaps be said that whenever the verb is not, as it usually is, in the plural, it is followed by *with.*
>>>

<<<
forge makes -geable; see MUTE E.
===
**forge** makes *-geable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
forget makes -itable; see -T-, -TT-.
===
**forget** makes *-ttable*; see ^^-T-^^, ^^-TT-^^.
>>>

<<<
forgive makes -vable; see MUTE E.
===
**forgive** makes *-vable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
forgo. See FOR-, FORE-.
===
**forgo.** See ^^FOR-^^, ^^FORE-^^.
>>>

<<<
forgot, as a past participle for the current forgotten, is now, except in uneducated speech, a deliberate archaism.
===
**forgot,** as a past participle for the current *forgotten,* is now, except in uneducated speech, a deliberate archaism.
>>>

<<<
forlorn hope is not an abstract phrase transferred by metaphor to a storming party, but has that concrete sense in its own right, & only gets the abstract sense of desperate chance &c. by misunderstanding.
===
**forlorn hope** is not an abstract phrase transferred by metaphor to a storming party, but has that concrete sense in its own right, & only gets the abstract sense of desperate chance &c. by misunderstanding.
>>>

<<<
Hope is not the English word, but is a mis-spelling of the Dutch hoop = English heap; the forlorn hope is the devoted or lost band, those who sacrifice themselves in leading the attack.
===
*Hope* is not the English word, but is a mis-spelling of the Dutch *hoop* = English *heap*; the forlorn hope is the devoted or lost band, those who sacrifice themselves in leading the attack.
>>>

<<<
The spelling of hope once fixed, the mistake was inevitable; but it is well to keep the original meaning in mind; see TRUE & FALSE ETYMOLOGY.
===
The spelling of *hope* once fixed, the mistake was inevitable; but it is well to keep the original meaning in mind; see ^^TRUE^^ & ^^FALSE ETYMOLOGY^^.
>>>

<<<
formalism, formality. It is only from the more abstract sense of formality, from formality as the name
===
**formalism, formality.** It is only from the more abstract sense of *formality*, from *formality* as the name
>>>

<<<
+ of a quality & not of an action, that formalism requires to be distinguished; & there, while formality means the observance of forms,
===
+ of a quality & not of an action, that *formalism* requires to be distinguished; & there, while *formality* means the observance of forms,
>>>

<<<
formalism is the disposition to use them & belief in their importance; formality is the outward sign of formalism; see -ISM & -ITY. FORMAL WORDS. There are large
===
*formalism* is the disposition to use them & belief in their importance; formality is the outward sign of formalism; see ^^-ISM^^ & ^^-ITY^^.

**FORMAL WORDS.** There are large
>>>

<<<
We tell our thoughts, like our children, to put on their hats & coats before they go out; we want the window shut, but we ask if our fellow passenger would
===
We tell our thoughts, like our children, to put on their hats & coats before they go out; we want the window *shut*, but we ask if our fellow passenger would
>>>

<<<
mind its being closed;\ we think of our soldiers as plucky fellows, but call them in the bulletins valiant troops.
===
mind its being *closed*; we think of our soldiers as *plucky fellows*, but call them in the bulletins *valiant troops*.
>>>

<<<
to the airman it comes natural to think & talk of his bus, but he does well to call it in print by another name.
===
to the airman it comes natural to think & talk of his *bus*, but he does well to call it in print by another name.
>>>

<<<
What is intended in this article is not to protest against all change of the indoor into the outdoor word,
===
What is intended in this article is not to protest against *all* change of the indoor into the outdoor word,
>>>

<<<
into these:—accommodation (room); announce (give out); bear (carry); cast (throw); cease (stop); close (shut); collation (meal); comestibles
===
into these:—*accommodation* (room); *announce* (give out); *bear* (carry); *cast* (throw); *cease* (stop); *close* (shut); *collation* (meal); *comestibles*
>>>
